 And please share with your friends. So moving forward, we will now go into the appendicular skeleton.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 So we've completed the axial skeleton. Now we're going to talk about all the bones that are off to the side that are lateral to the axial skeleton.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 So the two main girdles that your appendicular skeleton consists of are the pectoral girdle and the pelvic girdle.

8
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 Now everything, the pectoral girdle, it attaches all your upper body limbs to the trunk.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
 And the pelvic girdle, it attaches all your lower body limbs to the trunk.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:08,000
 So we're going to first be talking about the pectoral girdle. This is also known as your shoulder girdle.

11
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
 So the pectoral girdle, again, another name for it is the shoulder girdle.

12
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:21,000
 The bones that are made up of are your clavicles, which are coming in as the collar bones anteriorly,

13
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 and then your shoulder blade, or more specifically, the correct anatomical term for that is the scapula posteriorly.

14
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 So both clavicles and scapulae, these are the two bones that form the pectoral girdle.

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:42,000
 Now, in addition to these bones, the muscles that are associated, that are attached to these bones,

16
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 the combination of these things, these are what forms your shoulders.

17
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 Now, anteriorly, the medial end of the clavicles, they are articulated with the sternum.

18
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 And the distal end of the clavicle, they meet the scapula laterally.

19
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
 Now, the scapula, it's not attached to the axial skeleton.

20
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 It actually, it's held in place by muscles that are attached to it, that form the shoulder joint.

21
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,000
 And this is one of the reasons why it has such a huge range of motion.

22
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
 So only the clavicle attaches to the axial skeleton.

23
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 The scapula, this allows the scapula to move more freely across the thorax.

24
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
 And again, this is how you end up getting a lot of motion in your arm.

25
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 Now, the socket, the other part of it is the socket of the shoulder joint, it's called the glenoid cavity.

26
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 Now, it's a shallow and it's very poorly reinforced.

27
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
 So one of the downsides of that is that you can dislocate your upper arm quite easily from it.

28
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,000
 However, the other part of it is because of this glenoid cavity.

29
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 Now, it's circular in nature.

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 And when you look at your upper arm, excuse me, when you look at your upper arm,

31
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 it has a socket that's also circular.

32
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:09,000
 And because of this, this is how you end up getting a very wide range of motion and also a lot of flexibility.

33
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:19,000
 Be sure you view this video, this animation at the N.P., the website, if you have online access,

34
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 or when you purchase the book, there's a DVD in your book, be sure you look at this.

35
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 This will be very helpful for you to understanding the movements that you have at the petrol griddle.

36
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
 Now, so here you go, this is the clavicle, this is your collarbone.

37
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:41,000
 So notice, immediately, it's attached or anchored to the manubrum of your sternum.

38
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
 And again, laterally, it articulates with the scapula.

39
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
 Now, this is this glenoid cavity of the scapula.

40
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 And this glenoid cavity is what articulates with the head of this humerus.

41
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
 This is your upper arm bone.

42
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
 And again, it's because of this cavity, and also because of the fact that this shoulder griddle,

43
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 it's not anchored posteriorly to your vertebral column that you end up having all the flexibility

44
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
 and this incredible range of motion of your upper arm.

45
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
 And this is a chromium end of the scapula.

46
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:26,000
 So this is where these two, the scapula and the clavicle meet is this acromial clavicular joint.

47
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
 The acromial clavicular joint.

48
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
 Now, the clavicles, it's commonly referred to as the collarbones.

49
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
 This is an S-shaped bone.

50
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
 Now, the sternal end, it articulates, as you saw in the previous picture, with the sternum,

51
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
 with the manubrum of the sternum, medially.

52
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 And then it's the flattened acromial end of this clavicle.

53
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,000
 It articulates laterally with the scapula.

54
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
 The medial tutors of the clavicle, it's convex anteriorly.

55
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
 It's lateral third is concave anteriorly.

56
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,000
 Now, its superior surface is quite smooth, but when you look at the inferior surface,

57
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 it's rigid and grooved because of the ligaments and by the muscles that attach to it.

58
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
 So the trapezoid line and the conid tubercle, these are anchoring points for a ligament

59
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 which runs to attach to the scapula.

60
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 So aside from anchoring many muscles, the clavicle also acts as a brace.

61
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
 So they hold the scapula and arms out laterally away from the narrow superior part of the thorax.

62
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
 Now, this bracing function becomes obvious when the clavicle is fractured.

63
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
 Your entire shoulder region, it collapses medially.

64
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:48,000
 The clavicle also transforms compression forces from your upper limbs to the axial skeleton.

65
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:53,000
 Now, the clavicle, it's not a very strong bone and it's likely to become fractured,

66
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:58,000
 especially when you're falling down and you stretch your arm out to break the fall.

67
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:04,000
 There's a high probability, again, depending on other factors,

68
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
 you know, a person's weight, how high you're falling from, how much force you're falling down with,

69
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,000
 and, again, the position of your arm and the body when you hit the ground.

70
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
 But, yeah, that clavicle can fracture.

71
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
 Now, with that said, it is a fragile bone.

72
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,000
 But in individuals that work out a lot, people that are lifting weights

73
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:30,000
 or people that are doing a lot of manual labor, you notice that this bone actually becomes larger.

74
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:35,000
 And, again, it's part of the bone, remember, it starts to thicken, remember what we learned about bones.

75
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:39,000
 So, it starts to thicken, but then also the muscles, they're also growing.

76
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 So, you know, the combination of both the larger muscles and the bone being thicker,

77
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:50,000
 that makes it a little bit more stronger.

78
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:54,000
 So, here's the bone, this is your clavicle right over here.

79
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:02,000
 So, this is a sternal end, this is the part that's going to be articulating immediately with the sternum.

80
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:09,000
 And this is the acromial end, that's going to be articulating with the scapula laterally.

81
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:14,000
 So, again, notice the shape of it, all right, how it is, and notice that this part starts to flatten out,

82
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
 while this part stays more rounded.

83
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:22,000
 Now, as you go to the other side, when you look at this inferior review,

84
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:27,000
 you can see these, the trapezoid lines very clearly.

85
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
 Also, you can see this conoid tube look that we talked about.

86
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:36,000
 And these are where ligaments are going to be attaching to hold the scapula, to attach the scapula.

87
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
 Now, the next bone that we're going to be looking at is the scapula.

88
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
 So, the scapula, it's also called the shoulder blade.

89
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,000
 It's a thin triangular flat bone that we find on the dorsal surface of your rib cage

90
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
 between the second and seventh costals.

91
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:56,000
 Now, each scapula, it has three borders.

92
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:59,000
 The superior border is the shortest and sharpest border.

93
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,000
 The medial, or it's also known as the vertebral border, it parallels the vertebral column.

94
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:11,000
 Now, the thick lateral or axillary border, this is the part that you find right at your armpits,

95
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:16,000
 and it ends superiorly in a small shallow fossa that we call the glenoid cavity.

96
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,000
 And it's within this glenoid cavity that the head of the humerus, which is your upper arm bone,

97
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:28,000
 will be articulating at the scapula's three corners or angles.

98
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,000
 The superior scapula border meets the medial border at the superior angle

99
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
 and the lateral border at the lateral angle.

100
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
 Now, the medial and lateral borders, they join at the inferior angle.

101
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:43,000
 And the inferior angle, it moves extensively as the arm is raised and lowered.

102
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:51,000
 Now, let's look at some of the features of the bone that we find on the scapula.

103
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,000
 Now, the anterior or the coastal surface of the scapula, it's concave and it's relatively featureless.

104
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:01,000
 It's the posterior surface of the scapula that we find in the landmarks.

105
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,000
 Now, the very first thing that you're going to see is a prominent spine.

106
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
 Now, you can also feel this very easily.

107
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,000
 If you reach back and you go just touching your shoulder area

108
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:19,000
 and you feel that big bump that's present, that's the spine of your scapula.

109
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 Now, the spine, it ends laterally in an enlarged, rough and triangular projection that's called the acromion.

110
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
 Now, the acromion, it articulates with the acromial end of the clavicle.

111
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
 And this is what forms this acromial clavicular joint that we looked at a few slides ago.

112
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,000
 Now, projecting anteriorly from the superior scapular border, we have the corticoid process.

113
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:42,000
 Now, the corticoid process, it helps anchor the bicep muscles of your arm.

114
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:50,000
 And when you flex your arm, again, the muscle that shortens and pops up, that's your bicep muscle.

115
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:59,000
 Now, it's bounded by the super scapular notch and by the glenoid cavity laterally.

116
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
 Now, the super scapular notch, it serves as a nerve passage.

117
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:12,000
 We also see several large fossa or fossae that appear on both the anterior and posterior aspects of the scapula.

118
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
 And they're named according to their location.

119
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:22,000
 So, the infraspinus and the supraspinus fossa, these are inferior and superior located to the spine.

120
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:30,000
 Now, the subscapular fossa, this is a shallow concavity that's formed by the entire anterior scapular surface.

121
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:38,000
 Now, lying within these fossae, we find muscles that have the bare names of these fossae.

122
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:43,000
 So, here's the scapula, and this is the right scapula.

123
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
 And you're looking at the anterior, this is an anterior view.

124
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,000
 So, this entire region, this is a subscapular fossa.

125
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
 Now, because this is the right scapula, this is going to be the medial border.

126
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
 This is the inferior angle, and then this is a superior angle.

127
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
 So, you have a superior and inferior angle.

128
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
 Now, this is the lateral border.

129
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:11,000
 Remember, lateral border, this is the right scapula, and you can find out, again, so if you put yourself,

130
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:22,000
 if you take this and you flip it around backwards, if you flip it around at 180 degrees,

131
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
 and then you're going to end up putting it to yourself.

132
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
 So, you'll see that this is going to be where your right arm is.

133
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:33,000
 So, this part, this is where the head of your humerus is going to be attaching to you over here.

134
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:37,000
 Now, this is your acromionin, this is the coracoid process.

135
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 This is a glenoid cavity.

136
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
 This is the suprascapular notch.

137
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
 This is a superior border.

138
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
 Now, let's look at, there you go.

139
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
 This is the posterior view of this right scapula.

140
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:49,000
 So, there you go.

141
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
 So, as you're looking at it, this is how it's going to be positioned in your body.

142
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:00,000
 So, when you take your left arm and you, you know, you feel the back of your shoulder,

143
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
 and this is that shoulder blade that you call.

144
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:03,000
 This is that spine.

145
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:04,000
 Okay?

146
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:08,000
 Now, this is a supraspinous fossa, and this is the infraspinous fossa.

147
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
 So, this is the spine.

148
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,000
 So, above the spine, super below infra.

149
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
 This is the medial border.

150
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,000
 This is the lateral border.

151
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:19,000
 And again, this is that acromion end, and this is the coracoid process.

152
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,000
 And you can also see the suprascapular notch over here.

153
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:31,000
 So, once again, this is a lateral view of this scapula.

154
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
 So, notice over here you had this infraglinoid tubercle.

155
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:39,000
 So, this is going to be an attation point for a muscle.

156
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:42,000
 This is an inferior angle.

157
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
 The subscapular fossa over here.

158
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
 The infra spinous fossa is over there.

159
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
 You can see the glenoid cavity.

160
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:53,000
 So, this is where you're the head of your upper arm bone, the head of the humerus.

161
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
 This is where it's going to be articulating at over here.

162
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:01,000
 You have the coracoid process right there, and the chromion end over there.

163
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
 Let's see.

164
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,000
 All right.

165
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:09,000
 Now we're going to be going and looking at the bones that make up your upper limb.

166
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:16,000
 So, there's 30 bones that form the framework of each of your upper limbs.

167
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:23,000
 So, in general, the three main bones are the arms, the forearm arms, and then your hand bones.

168
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
 And this is where you find the most number of bones in our hand.

169
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
 Now, the upper arm is made up of the humerus.

170
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:34,000
 This is a long bone, and it articulates with your scapula.

171
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
 Then you have your forearm.

172
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:43,000
 Now, your forearm, it consists of two bones, your radius and ulna.

173
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:52,000
 Now, when you stick your hands outwards, again, in the anatomical position, the bone that's on the side of your thumb.

174
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:58,000
 The bone that runs on the same side as your thumb does, that's your radius.

175
00:13:58,000 --> 00:13:59,000
 Okay?

176
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:05,000
 And then the bone that runs along the same side as your pinky does, this is your ulna.

177
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,000
 Okay?

178
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,000
 So, you have your radius and ulna.

179
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:16,000
 So, remember, a little finger, a little finger, you have ulna, and your thumb, you have the radius.

180
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
 Then you have the bones that make up your hand.

181
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:23,000
 Your hand is made up of eight bones that make up your wrist, which are called the carples.

182
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:29,000
 Then you have five bones that make up the bones that make up your palms, which are the metacarpals.

183
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:35,000
 And then you have 14 bones that make up your fingers, which are called the phalanges.

184
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,000
 Now, the humerus is the only bone that makes up your upper arm.

185
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
 It's the largest and longest bone of your upper limb.

186
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:48,000
 It articulates superiorly with the glenoid cavity of the scapula, and inferiorly, it articulates with the radius and the ulna.

187
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
 Now, these are the two lower arm bones.

188
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:57,000
 Now, remember, the radius is on the thumb side, and the ulna is on your pinky, your little finger side.

189
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:05,000
 These are very common test questions that make sure you don't forget this, stressing this a few times, so you guys take note of that.

190
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:11,000
 Now, let's look at some of the features of your humerus.

191
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,000
 Now, at the proximal end of the humerus, you find this smooth spherical head.

192
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
 Now, this is a part that fits into the glenoid cavity of the scapula.

193
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:28,000
 Now, because of the shape of this head and the structure of the glenoid cavity,

194
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:33,000
 this is what allows this entire bone to hang freely at the sides of your body.

195
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:39,000
 Now, immediately inferior to the head, we have this constriction, and this is called the anatomical neck.

196
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:45,000
 Now, inferior to this, we have the lateral aspect.

197
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:50,000
 We have this greater tubercle, and on the medial aspect, we have this lesser tubercle.

198
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:58,000
 Now, between the greater and the lesser tubercle, we find this intertubicular sulcus.

199
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:01,000
 Another preferred is this bicepital groove.

200
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:06,000
 Now, these tubercles, these are sites for attachments of your rotator cuff muscles.

201
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:14,000
 The intertubicular sulcus, it guides the tendon of the bicep muscles of your arms to attach it to the point

202
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:22,000
 at the rim of the glenoid cavity, which is called the supraglenoid tubercle.

203
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:34,000
 Now, notice that these names, they describe the location, the named after the location.

204
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:39,000
 So, supraglenoid, okay, above the glenoid, the tubercle they find above the glenoid cavity,

205
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
 supraglenoid tubercle.

206
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:46,000
 Now, just distal to the tubercle, we have the surgical neck.

207
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:58,000
 Now, it's named the surgical neck because this is a site where most frequently we find the fractures of this bone take place.

208
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:09,000
 Now, as we continue to move along this bone, now midway down the shaft, on the lateral aspect, we have this V-shaped deltoid tuberosity.

