 Whereas in the males, the issue tuberosity is a much longer, sharper and more, they quite more medially as well.

767
00:56:53,000 --> 00:56:55,000
 Now we're going to be looking at the lower limb.

768
00:56:55,000 --> 00:56:59,000
 Now the lower limb, it carries the entire weight of your body when you're standing up.

769
00:56:59,000 --> 00:57:04,000
 Now it's subjected to exceptional forces when you're walking, when you're jumping, when you're running.

770
00:57:04,000 --> 00:57:06,000
 There's three parts of the lower limb.

771
00:57:06,000 --> 00:57:09,000
 Your thigh, leg and foot.

772
00:57:09,000 --> 00:57:10,000
 Now the femur.

773
00:57:10,000 --> 00:57:11,000
 This is the first part.

774
00:57:11,000 --> 00:57:14,000
 This is the largest and it's the strongest bone in the body.

775
00:57:14,000 --> 00:57:18,000
 And it makes up for about one quarter of a person's height.

776
00:57:18,000 --> 00:57:22,000
 Now it articulates proximally at the acetabulum of your hip.

777
00:57:22,000 --> 00:57:26,000
 And distally, it articulates with the tibia and the patella.

778
00:57:26,000 --> 00:57:29,000
 The patella, this is more common known as your knee bone, your kneecap.

779
00:57:29,000 --> 00:57:37,000
 It's a seismite bone that we find in the quadriceps tendon and it helps protect this knee joint.

780
00:57:37,000 --> 00:57:41,000
 Now we're going to be looking at some of the features of your femur, your thigh bone.

781
00:57:41,000 --> 00:57:46,000
 Now the bone like head of the femur, it has a small central pit that's called the fovea capidus.

782
00:57:46,000 --> 00:57:54,000
 Now the short ligament of the head of the femur runs from this pit to the acetabulum where it helps secure the femur in place.

783
00:57:54,000 --> 00:57:58,000
 The head is carried on a neck that angles laterally to the shaft.

784
00:57:58,000 --> 00:58:04,000
 This arrangement reflects the fact that the femur articulates with the lateral aspect of the pelvis.

785
00:58:04,000 --> 00:58:09,000
 The neck is the weakest part of the femur and is most often where fractures occur.

786
00:58:09,000 --> 00:58:16,000
 Now at the junction of the shaft and neck, we have the lateral greater trochanter and the posterior medial lesser trochanter.

787
00:58:16,000 --> 00:58:21,000
 Now these projections serve as sites for your thigh and butt muscles to attach to you.

788
00:58:21,000 --> 00:58:27,000
 Now the two trochanters, they're connected by the inter trochanic line anteriorly

789
00:58:27,000 --> 00:58:31,000
 and by the prominent inter trochanic crest posteriorly.

790
00:58:31,000 --> 00:58:37,000
 Now inferior to the inter trochanic crest on the posterior shaft, we have the gluteal tuberosity.

791
00:58:37,000 --> 00:58:44,000
 Now the gluteal tuberosity, it blends into a long vertical ridge called the linear aspra inferiorly.

792
00:58:44,000 --> 00:58:47,000
 Now distally, the linear aspra, it diverges.

793
00:58:47,000 --> 00:58:52,000
 It forms the medial and lateral supercondular lines.

794
00:58:52,000 --> 00:58:55,000
 Now all these markings, these are sites for muscles to attach to you.

795
00:58:55,000 --> 00:59:00,000
 Now distally, the femur broadens and ends in the lateral medial condiles.

796
00:59:00,000 --> 00:59:04,000
 Now these condiles, you're articulated with the tibia of the leg.

797
00:59:04,000 --> 00:59:09,000
 Now the medial lateral epicondiles, they flank the condiles superiorly.

798
00:59:09,000 --> 00:59:13,000
 So on the superior part of the medial epicondile is a bump.

799
00:59:13,000 --> 00:59:18,000
 Now this is called the adductor tubercle.

800
00:59:18,000 --> 00:59:24,000
 The smooth patellar surface between the condiles and the anterior femoral surface, they articulate with the patella.

801
00:59:24,000 --> 00:59:27,000
 And again, this is your kneecap.

802
00:59:27,000 --> 00:59:35,000
 Now between the condiles on the posterior aspect of the femur, we have this deep u-shaped intercondular fossa.

803
00:59:35,000 --> 00:59:41,000
 And superior to that on the shaft, we have this smooth popliteal surface.

804
00:59:41,000 --> 00:59:42,000
 So here we go.

805
00:59:42,000 --> 00:59:47,000
 Now this is the patella over here, and more commonly your kneecap.

806
00:59:47,000 --> 00:59:50,000
 Again, so this is an anterior view, this is the posterior view.

807
00:59:50,000 --> 00:59:56,000
 So you can see over here, the lateral condyle, the femur, and this is the facet for the medial condyle, the femur.

808
00:59:56,000 --> 00:59:58,000
 So where it's going to articulate with.

809
00:59:58,000 --> 01:00:05,000
 Notice how smooth it is over here, but on the anterior, the posterior aspect of it, but the anterior aspect is quite bumpy

810
01:00:05,000 --> 01:00:07,000
 and with a lot of the ridges in this way.

811
01:00:07,000 --> 01:00:13,000
 Again, you have the muscles and the tendons that are attached to it to hold it in place.

812
01:00:13,000 --> 01:00:19,000
 And here you have your femur, this is the anterior view, this is the posterior view.

813
01:00:19,000 --> 01:00:21,000
 So this is the head of the femur.

814
01:00:21,000 --> 01:00:24,000
 And right over here, this is the fovea capidus that you see here.

815
01:00:24,000 --> 01:00:27,000
 This is the neck of the femur over here.

816
01:00:27,000 --> 01:00:31,000
 Now this is your greater trochanter, this is the lesser trochanter.

817
01:00:31,000 --> 01:00:35,000
 And between the greater and the lesser trochanter, we have this inter trochanteric line.

818
01:00:35,000 --> 01:00:42,000
 Now this is the gluteal tuberosity, and this is the linear aspera that you see over here.

819
01:00:42,000 --> 01:00:52,000
 As you said, the linear aspera, it bifurcates into the medial, the medial and the lateral supracondylar lines.

820
01:00:52,000 --> 01:00:58,000
 And as we go down, we have the intracondylar fossa over here that we see in the posterior aspect.

821
01:00:58,000 --> 01:01:02,000
 And then this is your lateral condyle, and this is the medial condyle.

822
01:01:02,000 --> 01:01:07,000
 And this is the adductor tubercle that you see right over here, the adductor tubercle.

823
01:01:07,000 --> 01:01:08,000
 There it is again over here.

