01:01:39,000 --> 01:01:46,000
 All right, so moving downwards, we're going to be looking at the next two bonds, which are the tibia and the fibula.

833
01:01:46,000 --> 01:01:52,000
 And then we're going to be looking at your metatarsals and phalanges.

834
01:01:52,000 --> 01:01:54,000
 And that should be about it.

835
01:01:54,000 --> 01:02:02,000
 So moving forward, so your leg, your lower leg, it's made up of the two parallel bonds, the tibia,

836
01:02:02,000 --> 01:02:04,000
 and the fibula.

837
01:02:04,000 --> 01:02:12,000
 Now, the tibia, this is the much thicker and stronger of the two bonds, and it's located medially.

838
01:02:12,000 --> 01:02:18,000
 Now, the fibula, this is the bone that's located more lateral.

839
01:02:18,000 --> 01:02:20,000
 It's located lateral to the tibia.

840
01:02:20,000 --> 01:02:22,000
 The fibula, it's not a weight-bearing bone.

841
01:02:22,000 --> 01:02:24,000
 It doesn't articulate with the femur.

842
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:28,000
 It articulates proximally and distally with the tibia.

843
01:02:28,000 --> 01:02:33,000
 Several muscles, they originate from the fibula, so again, it serves as attachment points.

844
01:02:33,000 --> 01:02:37,000
 Its job more is to help stabilize the tibia also.

845
01:02:37,000 --> 01:02:41,000
 So it kind of helps prevent you from kind of tipping over.

846
01:02:41,000 --> 01:02:44,000
 Now, the tibia, this is the medial of the two leg bones.

847
01:02:44,000 --> 01:02:50,000
 And this is the bone that received the weight from the femur and the transmittes to the foot.

848
01:02:50,000 --> 01:02:52,000
 So this is the stronger of the two bones, as I said.

849
01:02:52,000 --> 01:02:56,000
 Now, both of these bones, they're connected by this intra-RCS membrane.

850
01:02:56,000 --> 01:02:59,000
 Again, this is connective tissue.

